# RedLine Stealer (2020 onwards). Sold as a service; spread through fake "cracked" software and fake downloads.
# A quest (QUESTS.md): the trainee works a poster deadline, installs the licensed editor safely, then meets RedLine in the "free" pack.
# There is no record of one real victim's job: Dana is an invented marketing coordinator, as many stealer victims were ordinary staff.
os windows
user Dana Okafor
about
  | A "free" copy of paid software was really RedLine, a password stealer sold to criminals as a service. In seconds it copied saved browser passwords, cookies (which keep you signed in), card details and crypto wallets, and sent them to its buyer.
  | It was spread through video descriptions, search ads and "crack" sites, as a password-protected archive that scanners cannot look inside until it is unpacked. This quest invents the victim: a marketing coordinator racing a print deadline.
story
  | You are Dana Okafor, marketing coordinator at Northwind Traders. This week's autumn sale needs ten posters, and the print shop, Corner Print, takes the last file at 5 pm.
  | It is mid-afternoon. You have 8 of the 10 posters ready for print. Two to go.
  | Your browser remembers your print-shop and social-media passwords. You have also copied them into two text files on the desktop: a bad habit.
quota 8 10 Posters ready for print

# Dana's desk: logins she copies from (soft flags: careless, but only here to keep the training simple).
file Desktop/print-shop-login.txt
  at 930 200
  | Corner Print account (training copy)
  | Login: dana.okafor@northwind.example
  | Password: Poster-Fall-2026!
file Desktop/social-media-logins.txt
  at 930 300
  | Northwind shared social accounts (training copy)
  | Login: northwind.marketing
  | Password: Sale-Season#7
  | Backup code: 5521 0987

# The company's own software portal, where the licensed editor really lives.
page portal software.northwind.example/photo-editor-pro
  title Northwind Software Portal: Photo Editor Pro
  brand Northwind IT
  heading Photo Editor Pro 2025 (company licence)
  | A seat is reserved for Dana Okafor. Install it on your work computer.
  section About this download
  row Publisher = PixelForge Software Ltd (verified)
  row Licence = Northwind company licence, 1 seat
  row Version = 2025.2, 118 MB
  button Download installer

program pf Photo Editor Pro installer
  publisher PixelForge Software Ltd
  signed yes
  prompt elevation
  description Photo Editor Pro installer

mail seat
  from IT Helpdesk <it.helpdesk@northwind.example>
  subject Your Photo Editor Pro seat is ready
  link portal
  | Hi Dana,
  |
  | Your seat for Photo Editor Pro is ready. Install it from the company software portal with the link below.
  |
  | IT Helpdesk

step Poster 9 of 10
  look Mail: IT's message about your photo editor
  | You asked IT for the photo editor, and it has set you up with a licensed copy. Install it the normal way, so you know what normal looks like.
notify mail IT Helpdesk
  | Your Photo Editor Pro seat is ready

step The link goes to the portal
  look the link under IT's message
  when mail seat
  | The link goes to software.northwind.example, the company's own address. Hover over a link before you click it, to check where it goes.
open seat

step The company portal
  look the address bar, and the publisher line on the page
  when visit portal
  move Press “Download installer”.
  | The portal is on the company's own address, and the page names the publisher. Real software comes with a name behind it.
open portal

step Saved to Downloads
  look the installer in Downloads
  when choose portal 1
  | The installer is in Downloads. Open it: your computer will ask permission. Read what it asks before you agree.
file Downloads/PixelForge_PhotoEditorPro_Setup.exe
  program pf
  size 118000000
notify browser PixelForge_PhotoEditorPro_Setup.exe
  | Download complete
open Downloads

step A named publisher
  look the permission box: the program is the one the portal named
  when prompt pf
  | Your computer asks permission to make changes, as it does for every installer. The portal named PixelForge Software as a verified publisher, and this installer is signed by it. Remember that: a signed installer gets no unknown-app warning.
open Downloads/PixelForge_PhotoEditorPro_Setup.exe

step 9 of 10 done
  look the counter in the guide, then Mail: Marcus's new message
  when run pf
  | Installed. Poster 9 is done, with nobody asking you to turn anything off. Marcus has just written about poster 10.
answer run
quota +1

# The lure: a "free full version" with a password-protected zip.
page crack free-software-hub.invalid/photo-editor-pro
  title Photo Editor Pro 2026 FULL FREE (no virus)
  brand Free Software Hub
  heading Photo Editor Pro 2026 FULL FREE (no virus)
  | Photo Editor Pro 2026 with the Pro effects pack, free. 48,201 downloads. Works every time!
  | Download PhotoEditorPro_2026_FULL.zip (10 MB)
  | Archive password: 2026
  | Turn off your antivirus first, it is a false positive.
  section Comments
  item works perfectly, thanks!!
  item 10/10 saved me 300 dollars
  item the password is 2026, just disable your antivirus
  button Download
view redline-error message
  title PhotoEditorPro Setup
  icon error
  | The code execution cannot proceed because MSVCP140.dll was not found. Reinstalling the program may fix this problem.
  button OK -> close primary
program redline PhotoEditorPro_Setup.exe
  publisher none
  signed no
  prompt reputation
  prompt elevation
  description Photo Editor Pro installer
  process AppLaunch.exe
  cpu 26
  mem 64
  parent PhotoEditorPro_Setup.exe
  command C:\Windows\Microsoft.NET\Framework\v4.0.30319\AppLaunch.exe
  selfdelete
mail pack
  from Marcus Lee <marcus.lee@northwind.example>
  subject Poster 10 needs the effects pack
  link crack
  | Dana,
  |
  | Poster 10 needs the Pro effects pack for the recolour, and IT says that licence takes five working days. Corner Print closes at 5.
  |
  | I did a quick search and this page came up. Can you see whether it does what we need?
  |
  | Marcus
notify mail Marcus Lee
  | Poster 10 needs the effects pack

step Poster 10 needs more
  look Marcus's message, and the link under it
  when mail pack
  | Marcus needs an effects pack that IT cannot get today, and the print shop closes at 5. The link is his search result, not a company page.
  | Before you click it, hover over the link and read the address.
open pack

step A page that promises the pack
  look the address, and the description on the page
  when visit crack
  move Read the page, then press “Download”.
  | The address is free-software-hub.invalid: not the company portal and not the vendor. The page says the archive password is 2026 and to turn off your antivirus first.
  | Stealers like RedLine spread this way: pages and video descriptions, comments that say it works, and a "false positive" excuse.
close mail
open crack

step A zip scanners cannot look inside
  look the zip in Downloads, and the README and installer inside it
  when choose crack 1
  | The download is a password-protected zip, so scanners cannot look inside until it is unpacked. The README repeats the advice to switch protection off.
  | Unpacked, the installer is padded with empty data to 640 MB, a size some scanners skip. Open the installer.
notify browser PhotoEditorPro_2026_FULL.zip
  | Download complete
folder Downloads/PhotoEditorPro_2026_FULL.zip
file Downloads/PhotoEditorPro_2026_FULL.zip/README.txt
  | Password: 2026
  | Disable your antivirus before installing. It is a false positive.
  | Run PhotoEditorPro_Setup.
file Downloads/PhotoEditorPro_2026_FULL.zip/PhotoEditorPro_Setup.exe
  program redline
  size 640000000
open Downloads/PhotoEditorPro_2026_FULL.zip

step The computer asks again
  look the first warning: it does not name a verified publisher
  when prompt redline
  move Click through the warnings until it runs. Windows: More info, then Run anyway, then Yes. A Mac: Open Anyway, then OK.
  | Last time the installer was signed by a verified publisher. This app is not: your computer does not recognise it, and asks whether to open it.
  | Most victims clicked through anyway.
  cue dialog-btn:run dialog:reputation:redline
open Downloads/PhotoEditorPro_2026_FULL.zip/PhotoEditorPro_Setup.exe

step Nothing useful installs
  look the error box, then AppLaunch.exe in Task Manager behind it
  when run redline
  cue view-btn:close mail:m-alert app:mail
  | The installer shows an error and closes: “MSVCP140.dll was not found”. Press OK.
  | Behind the error, RedLine ran inside a normal .NET tool called AppLaunch.exe. In a few seconds it read the browser's saved passwords and cookies, any card or wallet files it found, then deleted the installer.
  | It is still running in the background, under the name AppLaunch.exe.
answer run
answer run
open taskmanager
dialog message redline-error

# The payoff: the stolen cookie signs the attacker in. Here it takes minutes; real victims often found out days later.
page confirmed-clean corner-print.example/orders/4471/done
  title Order confirmed
  brand Corner Print
  heading Order 4471 confirmed
  | Other sessions were signed out and the poster is printing. Next: change your passwords from a different device.
page confirmed-open corner-print.example/orders/4471/done-open
  title Order confirmed
  brand Corner Print
  heading Order 4471 confirmed
  | The poster is printing. The unknown device is still signed in to your account.
page order-4471 corner-print.example/orders/4471
  title Corner Print: confirm order 4471
  brand Corner Print
  favorite
  heading Confirm order 4471
  | Northwind Traders, Autumn Sale poster 10 (A2, 50 copies). It prints as soon as you confirm.
  section Order
  row Poster = Autumn Sale poster 10 (A2)
  row Copies = 50
  row Pay with = Northwind account on file
  section Where your account is signed in
  row This computer = Northwind office, now
  section Other devices
  tone bad
  row Unknown device = Browser at 203.0.113.70, signed in 2 minutes ago
  note A session stays signed in on a device until it is signed out.
  button Sign out all other sessions, then confirm -> confirmed-clean
  button Confirm order -> confirmed-open
mail alert
  from Corner Print <orders@corner-print.example>
  subject Confirm order 4471: new sign-in to your account
  | Hi Dana,
  |
  | Poster 10 is ready to print once you confirm it in your Corner Print account. Before you do: we saw a sign-in from a device we do not recognise. If that was not you, sign out other sessions first.
  |
  | Corner Print
notify mail Corner Print
  | Confirm order 4471: new sign-in to your account

step The print shop writes
  look Mail: Corner Print's message about your account
  when mail alert
  move Open Corner Print from the favourites bar, not from the email.
  | Corner Print has written about a sign-in to your account. Read the message, then open Corner Print from the favourites bar, not from the email.
open alert

step Who is signed in?
  look “Where your account is signed in”: two devices
  when visit order-4471
  move Compare the two devices, then press a button.
  | Corner Print lists every device signed in to your account. Compare the list with the devices you actually use.
  | The two buttons do different things to the other sessions.
open order-4471

step Poster 10 of 10, and what RedLine took
  look the counter, then the animation: everything RedLine copied after you ran the file
  when choose order-4471 any
  | The order is placed and the poster is on its way to print. If you pressed Confirm without signing out, the unknown device is still in your account.
  | The data goes to whoever bought RedLine. Stolen cookies let them sign in without a password. Real victims found out later, from alerts like this one.
quota +1
attack data-exfil
  | saved passwords: print shop, social
  | browser cookies: the signed-in print shop session
  | the two login text files on the desktop
  | card details and wallets, if there are any

flag hard link pack
  | The link leads to an unfamiliar download site, not the company portal or the vendor.
flag hard page crack
  | “Turn off your antivirus first” and a password for the archive: a real vendor never asks for this.
flag hard file Downloads/PhotoEditorPro_2026_FULL.zip/PhotoEditorPro_Setup.exe
  | An installer from an unknown source, padded to 640 MB and hidden in a password-protected zip.
flag hard dialog reputation redline
  | Your computer does not recognise this app: no verified publisher. The company installer was signed by a verified publisher.
flag hard process AppLaunch.exe
  | RedLine running inside a normal .NET tool, started by the installer, reading the browser's saved data.
flag hard mail alert
  | The first sign: a device you did not sign in with is already signed in to your account.
flag hard section order-4471 3
  | An unknown device is signed in as you, with no password asked. The stolen cookie did that.
flag soft file Desktop/print-shop-login.txt
  | Passwords in a text file on the desktop: stealers copy desktop text files too.
flag soft file Desktop/social-media-logins.txt
  | Logins and backup codes belong in a password manager, not in a file.

lesson
  | An installer that asks you to switch off your antivirus is not an installer. The real one came from the company portal, from a verified publisher, and never asked.
  | “Free full version” downloads, password-protected zips and a fake “missing file” error are the stealer's usual costume. If a licence takes time, ask IT, not a search engine.
  | If you ran one: sign out of all sessions and change your passwords from another device, because stolen cookies skip the password.
